    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "A triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > is given in the plane with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/521f5839d6ba8b1a6a075bd53af4c3246ebeea64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = \\sqrt{7},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"81\" height=\"20\" > <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/d/aed738037e081c4c3e8caeb61af86cd6890d3ea8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = \\sqrt{13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1974d9e0973e21579e9fc53e7a440c55da70193.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA = \\sqrt{19},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" > circles are drawn with centers at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/6/5f642cbbd390b4882363ab6b5c2906dbf6be6cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/7/0f7cd02cb0937d26075c0512a6bfa50b2cdeaea4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"17\" height=\"37\" > <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/4/7547747b133453d3c209cff6cfbf129aa67a6947.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/f/5bf27edd6ee0c25241392922eca9b9c9144f4d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"15\" > respectively. Prove that there are points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/e/ace28869ca88b544cdd745a73cdfff3bf1c31302.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#039;,B&#039;,C&#039;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" > on these three circles respectively such that triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > is congruent to triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/b/a6b641c242b66290184ce4e40ad54861b8ddaa35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#039;B&#039;C&#039;.$\"  width=\"60\" height=\"14\" >",
    "post_canonical": "A triangle $ABC$ is given in the plane with $AB = \\sqrt{7},$ $BC = \\sqrt{13}$ and $CA = \\sqrt{19},$ circles are drawn with centers at $A,B$ and $C$ and radii $\\frac{1}{3},$ $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $1,$ respectively. Prove that there are points $A',B',C'$ on these three circles respectively such that triangle $ABC$ is congruent to triangle $A'B'C'.$",
